Well, not quite "genuine"; unless the level layout
code has changed since I last read it long ago, a
dead end passage is always pointed at _something
real_, but may fail to go far enough.  Essentially,
all of a level's rooms are laid out first, then a
passageway is started to connect some two rooms,
dodging around intervening rooms as needed, and is
constructed cell by cell from the starting room to
the ending room. It has a small chance per cell of
the passageway of failing to progress further. Over
a long passageway, such a failure happens fairly
frequently, so NetHack levels often have dead ends.
So, if you find a long dead end passageway, pointed
at nothing, try to find some alternate path to that
"nothing"; it's really a "something" that the
passageway failed to reach.

